      "sample_id": "31df8b0a0bde11eba7f7acde48001122",
      "question": "What is the place of birth of Anne Elizabeth Rector's husband?",
      "gold_answer": "Jersey City",
      "gold_doc_ids": [
        "Anne Elizabeth Rector\nAnne Elizabeth Rector (1899 – February 17, 1970) was an American artist. She was the daughter of Enoch J. Rector and she attended the Art Students League of New York studying under John French Sloan. Ann also studied landscape painting under Andrew Dasburg. She married Edmund Duffy and they moved to New York City in 1948, when her husband began work for the Saturday Evening Post. She later headed Rector Studios that manufactured glass top tables. Her daughter married Ivan Chermayeff, the son of Serge Ivan Chermayeff. Her childhood diaries were published in 2004. They had been found many years after Rector's death and described her life for the year of 1912.",
        "Edmund Duffy\nEdmund Duffy (March 1, 1899 – September 12, 1962), was an American editorial cartoonist. He grew up in Jersey City, New Jersey, eventually moving to metropolitan areas. Duffy did not attend high school, but instead went into the Art Students League of New York. Duffy's career took him to London, Paris, New York, and finally to Baltimore, where he spent the majority of his professional career working for \"The Baltimore Sun\". Duffy won three Pulitzer Prizes for Editorial Cartooning in 1931, 1934, and 1940. Duffy began working for the Baltimore Sun in 1924, when he was only about 25 years old, and he received high praise from the famous journalist H.L. Mencken."
      ],
      "retrieved_doc_ids": [
        "Devisingh Ransingh Shekhawat\nDevisingh Ransingh Shekhawat is the former and first First Gentleman of India as the husband of Pratibha Patil.",
        "Edmund Duffy\nEdmund Duffy (March 1, 1899 – September 12, 1962), was an American editorial cartoonist. He grew up in Jersey City, New Jersey, eventually moving to metropolitan areas. Duffy did not attend high school, but instead went into the Art Students League of New York. Duffy's career took him to London, Paris, New York, and finally to Baltimore, where he spent the majority of his professional career working for \"The Baltimore Sun\". Duffy won three Pulitzer Prizes for Editorial Cartooning in 1931, 1934, and 1940. Duffy began working for the Baltimore Sun in 1924, when he was only about 25 years old, and he received high praise from the famous journalist H.L. Mencken.",
        "Michael Rector\nMichael Rector( born December 16, 1993) is an American football wide receiver who is currently a free agent of the National Football League. He played college football at Stanford.",
        "Andrew Upton\nAndrew Upton( born 1 February 1966) is an Australian playwright, screenwriter, and director. He is the husband of actress Cate Blanchett.",
        "Anne Evans\nDame Anne Elizabeth Jane Evans,( born 20 August 1941) is an international British operatic soprano.",
        "Jon Leach\nJonathan Leach( born April 18, 1973) is a former professional tennis player from the United States. He is the husband of Lindsay Davenport.",
        "Stan Marks\nStan Marks is an Australian writer and journalist. He is the husband of Holocaust survivor Eva Marks.",
        "James Randall Marsh\nJames Randall Marsh( 1896- 1965) was an American artist and the husband of Anne Steele Marsh.",
        "Stan Rice\nStan Rice( November 7, 1942 – December 9, 2002) was an American poet and artist. He was the husband of author Anne Rice.",
        "Anne Elizabeth Rector\nAnne Elizabeth Rector (1899 – February 17, 1970) was an American artist. She was the daughter of Enoch J. Rector and she attended the Art Students League of New York studying under John French Sloan. Ann also studied landscape painting under Andrew Dasburg. She married Edmund Duffy and they moved to New York City in 1948, when her husband began work for the Saturday Evening Post. She later headed Rector Studios that manufactured glass top tables. Her daughter married Ivan Chermayeff, the son of Serge Ivan Chermayeff. Her childhood diaries were published in 2004. They had been found many years after Rector's death and described her life for the year of 1912."
      ]
