      "sample_id": "98f734a60bdd11eba7f7acde48001122",
      "question": "Where was the place of death of Amaury De Montfort (Died 1241)'s father?",
      "gold_answer": "Toulouse",
      "gold_doc_ids": [
        "Amaury de Montfort (died 1241)\nAmaury de Montfort (1192 – 1241) was the son of Simon de Montfort, 5th Earl of Leicester and Alix de Montmorency, and the brother of Simon de Montfort, 6th Earl of Leicester.",
        "Simon de Montfort, 5th Earl of Leicester\nSimon de Montfort, 5th Earl of Leicester (– 25 June 1218), known as Simon IV (or V) de Montfort and as Simon de Montfort the Elder, was a French nobleman and soldier who took part in the Fourth Crusade (1202–1204) and was a prominent leader of the Albigensian Crusade. He died at the Siege of Toulouse in 1218. He was lord of Montfort-l'Amaury in France and Earl of Leicester in England."
      ],
      "retrieved_doc_ids": [
        "Guillaume de Montfort of Hainaut\nGuillaume de Montfort, also known as Guillaume of Hainaut, was a French nobleman of the 10th and 11th centuries, the first Lord of Montfort- l' Amaury. He was succeeded as Lord of Montfort- l' Amaury by his son Amaury I de Montfort.",
        "John IV, Duke of Brittany\nJohn IV the Conqueror KG (in Breton Yann IV, in French Jean IV, and traditionally in English sources both John of Montfort and John V) (1339 – 1 November 1399) was Duke of Brittany and Count of Montfort from 1345 until his death and 7th Earl of Richmond from 1372 until his death.",
        "Beatrice, Countess of Montfort\nBeatrice de Montfort, Countess of Montfort -l' Amaury( c. December 1248 /1249 – 9 March 1312) was a ruling sovereign countess of Montfort from 1249 until 1312. She was also countess of Dreux by marriage to Robert IV of Dreux, Count of Dreux. She was the ancestor of the Dukes of Brittany from the House of Montfort- Dreux which derived its name from her title.",
        "John IV, Count of Soissons\nJohn IV( d. before May 1302), son of John III, Count of Soissons, and Marguerite of Montfort, daughter of Amaury VI, Count of Montfort. Count of Soissons, inherited upon his father ’s death in 1286. John married Marguerite of Rumigny, daughter of Hugh II of Rumigny and his wife Philippa. John and Marguerite had two children: Upon John ’s death in 1302, his son and namesake became count.",
        "Amaury de Montfort (died 1241)\nAmaury de Montfort (1192 – 1241) was the son of Simon de Montfort, 5th Earl of Leicester and Alix de Montmorency, and the brother of Simon de Montfort, 6th Earl of Leicester.",
        "Simon de Montfort, 5th Earl of Leicester\nSimon de Montfort, 5th Earl of Leicester (– 25 June 1218), known as Simon IV (or V) de Montfort and as Simon de Montfort the Elder, was a French nobleman and soldier who took part in the Fourth Crusade (1202–1204) and was a prominent leader of the Albigensian Crusade. He died at the Siege of Toulouse in 1218. He was lord of Montfort-l'Amaury in France and Earl of Leicester in England.",
        "Amaury II de Montfort\nAmaury II( d. 1089) was the fourth lord of Montfort l'Amaury. He was the son of Simon I, Lord of Montfort, and Isabel de Broyes. He succeeded his father and died soon after. He, in turn, was succeeded by his half brother Richard de Montfort.",
        "Philip of Montfort, Lord of Tyre\nPhilip Ι of Montfort,( d. 17 March 1270, Tyre) was Lord of La Ferté- Alais and Castres- en- Albigeois 1228 – 1270, Lord of Tyre 1246 – 1270, and Lord of Toron aft. 1240 – 1270. He was the son of Guy of Montfort and Helvis of Ibelin( daughter of Balian of Ibelin).",
        "Rudolf III von Montfort\nRudolf III von Montfort(* between 1260 and 1275; † 27 or 28 March 1334 in Arbon) was bishop of Chur( 1322–1325) and Konstanz( 1322 – 1334). He was born into the young family of Montfort- Feldkirch of the Swabian noble family of Montfort.",
        "Amaury I de Montfort\nAmaury I de Montfort( died) was Lord of Montfort, son of Guillaume de Montfort of Hainaut, the first Lord of Montfort. The castle of Montfort l'Amaury, of which he started the construction, was completed by his son Simon I de Montfort, who succeeded him as Lord of Montfort. He married Bertrade, whose origins are unknown. He and his wife had three children:"
      ]
